{"questions":[{"question_id":"1l31cqjjx","paperTitle":"IIT-JEE 2010 Paper 1 Offline","year":2010,"examDate":2010,"subject":"physics","chapter":"alternating-current","chapterGroup":"electricity","topic":"ac-circuits-and-power-in-ac-circuits","type":"mcq","marks":3,"negMarks":1,"permalink":"pan-ac-voltage-source-of-variable-angular-frequency-ome-jee-advanced-physics-alternating-current-6bxgvehskxe4ot8m","content":"<p>An AC voltage source of variable angular frequency <mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: -0.025ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"1.407ex\" height=\"1.027ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -443 622 454\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D714\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-I-1D714\"></use></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container> and fixed amplitude V<sub>0</sub> is connected in series with a capacitance C and an electric bulb of resistance R (inductance zero). When <mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: -0.025ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"1.407ex\" height=\"1.027ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -443 622 454\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D714\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-I-1D714\"></use></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container> is increased</p>","options":[{"identifier":"A","content":"the bulb glows dimmer."},{"identifier":"B","content":"the bulb glows brighter."},{"identifier":"C","content":"total impedance of the circuit is unchanged."},{"identifier":"D","content":"total impedance of the circuit increases."}],"correct_options":["B"],"explanation":"<p><img src